---
import AnalysisLayout from '../../layouts/AnalysisLayout.astro';
import RfmModel from '../../components/RfmModel.astro';
---

<AnalysisLayout
  title="Guest segmentation / campaign targeting model"
  description="Customer segmentation and budget optimisation for a restaurant loyalty campaign. RFM model, Solver optimisation and decision analysis over 18,095 transactions."
  heading="guest segmentation."
  course="Advanced Marketing Planning, MSc"
  year="2022"
  scope="individual project"
  tools={['Excel', 'Solver (GRG Nonlinear)', 'Pivot tables', 'VLOOKUP / COUNTIF / AVERAGEIF', 'RFM segmentation', 'Decision analysis']}
>

  <h2>the brief</h2>

  <p>
    A restaurant with a membership card scheme running since 2013 had a fixed marketing budget of <strong>€1,500</strong>
    and a database of past visits. Contacting one guest costs <strong>€5.75</strong>. The budget covers only a fraction
    of the customer base, so the question was not how to run the campaign but who to spend it on.
  </p>

  <p>
    The owner also had a second problem. Regulation on smoking in restaurants was expected to change and nobody knew
    which way. Any recommendation had to survive that uncertainty rather than assume it away.
  </p>

  <div class="rc-stats">
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">18,095</span><span class="rc-stat-l">transactions analysed</span></div>
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">2,320</span><span class="rc-stat-l">individual guests</span></div>
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">413</span><span class="rc-stat-l">days, Sep 2020 to Nov 2021</span></div>
    <div class="rc-stat"><span class="rc-stat-n">€1,500</span><span class="rc-stat-l">budget to allocate</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>approach</h2>

  <h3>1. Understand the base</h3>
  <p>
    I started with descriptive work: revenue by day and by month, visit counts by month and the ten highest-spending
    guests. Some of it was immediately useful. The busiest months were also the highest-revenue months, which meant
    volume rather than basket size was driving the business. One guest had visited 1,149 times and spent €35,630 in
    410 days, which told me the customer base was extremely uneven and that an average would be a misleading thing
    to plan around.
  </p>

  <h3>2. Segment with RFM</h3>
  <p>
    I scored every guest on three dimensions and split each into five bands using <code>VLOOKUP</code> against cutoff
    tables I set myself:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Recency</strong>, date of last visit</li>
    <li><strong>Frequency</strong>, number of visits, banded at 0, 3, 7, 11 and 19</li>
    <li><strong>Monetary</strong>, average spend, banded at 0, 23, 35, 49 and 61</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Then three parameters per segment: how many customers it holds (<code>COUNTIF</code>), their average response
    rate and their average value (both <code>AVERAGEIF</code>).
  </p>

  <h3>3. Build the economics</h3>
  <p>
    Expected profit from contacting one guest in segment <em>i</em> is
    <code>p<sub>i</sub>(V<sub>i</sub> − C) + (1 − p<sub>i</sub>)(−C)</code>, which simplifies to
    <code>p<sub>i</sub>V<sub>i</sub> − C</code>. You pay the contact cost whether or not they respond. Across a whole
    segment that becomes <code>N<sub>i</sub>(p<sub>i</sub>V<sub>i</sub> − C)</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    From there it is a constrained optimisation: choose what share <code>x<sub>i</sub></code> of each segment to
    target so that total expected profit is maximised and total spend stays inside €1,500. I ran it in Solver both
    ways, binary (target a segment or don't) and continuous (target part of a segment), for all three criteria and
    all three regulatory scenarios. Eighteen model configurations in total.
  </p>

  <h3>4. Test whether the answer holds</h3>
  <p>
    Rather than take the highest number and stop, I put all six candidate strategies against the three possible
    regulatory outcomes. The continuous version matched or beat its binary counterpart every time, so I carried the
    three continuous strategies forward and evaluated them four ways: average payoff, aggressive (best case),
    conservative (worst case) and minimax regret. Then I ran expected value across seven different probability
    weightings, from 0.4/0.3/0.3 through to treating the current rules as certain.
  </p>

  <h2>run it yourself</h2>

  <p>
    This is the model rebuilt in the browser, using the segment figures from the original workbook. Change the
    criterion, the scenario, the budget or the contact cost and the allocation resolves again.
  </p>

  <RfmModel />

  <h2>what came out of it</h2>

  <p>
    Segmenting by <strong>frequency</strong>, continuous, was the strongest option. The money goes to guests who have
    visited 19 or more times, funding that segment completely, with what remains going to 56% of the 11-to-18 visit
    segment. The whole budget gets used.
  </p>

  <div class="rc-result">
    <p class="rc-result-label">the part worth reading</p>
    <p>
      Frequency-continuous came first under <strong>all four decision criteria</strong>, carried <strong>zero
      regret in every regulatory scenario</strong> and ranked first at <strong>every one of the seven probability
      weightings</strong> I tested. The recommendation does not depend on guessing the regulation correctly.
    </p>
  </div>

  <p>
    That last point mattered more than the headline figure. A number that only works if you guess the legislation
    right is not much use to the person who has to sign off on the spend.
  </p>

  <p>
    The campaign I proposed followed from the analysis rather than from taste. Frequency was the criterion that
    paid, so reward frequency: recognise loyal guests directly. Monetary value scored worst of the three, so lift
    basket size separately, through an app where guests scan the bill and earn points against future visits, plus
    personalised offers tied to what someone actually orders.
  </p>

  <h2>tools</h2>
  <p>
    One Excel workbook, 42 sheets, 9 pivot tables and 18 saved Solver configurations. Everything from the raw
    transaction log through to the decision tree was built in it.
  </p>

  <p class="rc-note">
    Coming back to this workbook recently I audited all eighteen Solver sheets against each other. Thirteen are
    correct. On four of them, the unchanged-state frequency and monetary sheets, the objective cell points one column
    across at an empty reference where the contact cost should be, so those four report gross return instead of
    return net of spend. The headline figure for the chosen option reads €126,599.53 gross where it should read
    €125,099.53 net of the €1,500 spent. On the fifth, monetary continuous under the smoking ban, Solver was told to
    maximise one segment's cell instead of the total, so it stopped after funding a single segment and returned the
    binary answer. Solved properly it comes to about €35,216 rather than €18,212. Neither error changes the
    recommendation. Subtracting a constant leaves the segment ranking untouched and the corrected monetary figure
    still ranks last under every criterion, so frequency still wins everywhere and still carries zero regret. The
    interactive model above is built the corrected way and reports both figures.
  </p>

</AnalysisLayout>
